WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.960
Gillette sold blades stamped from a few cents of steel for 5 cents each, by replacing hand-forged

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:13.520
razors with high-volume progressive dies. This is First Principles Daily, episode 121. Before

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.500
Gillette, a man shaved with a straight razor that cost several dollars and required regular

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:25.020
professional honing. After 1904, the same man could buy a holder for 5 dollars and replace

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.800
the cutting edge itself for $0.5 per blade. The difference came from treating the

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.460
blade as a consumable made from thin high carbon steel rather than a precision

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:39.520
ground tool that lasted years. That single shift opened the path to per shave

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.260
costs that approached the price of the raw metal. Straight razors had been made

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:49.100
the same way for generations. A blacksmith or cutler started with a bar of high

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:54.400
carbon steel forged it to shape hardened it and then ground and honed the edge by

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.500
hand on progressively finer stones. Each razor was essentially unique and the edge dulled

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.460
after a few dozen shaves, sending the owner back to a barber or to his own straw and

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:11.360
home. Because the process was labor intensive and the finished tool was expected to last a lifetime,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.280
the price stayed high and the market stayed small. No one questioned the assumption that a razor was

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:23.540
a durable capital good whose value lay in the skill embedded in its edge. The forging step itself

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:29.560
required heating the steel bar to roughly 800 degrees Celsius, hammering it into a tapered profile,

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:33.920
and then annealing it slowly so the metal would not crack during later grinding.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:39.480
After hardening, the edge had to be brought to a bevel angle of about 15 to 20 degrees

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:45.720
through repeated passes on stones of decreasing grit, a sequence that could consume several hours per

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:51.620
razor. Retailers therefore treated the finished razor as an investment item whose purchase price

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:55.160
reflected both the material and the accumulated artisan time.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.980
Gillette began with the raw material, a narrow strip of high-carbon steel whose commodity

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.480
value was only a few cents per finished blade.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:09.320
That figure is the magic wand number, the cost if the atoms could be arranged instantly

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:10.440
into the final shape.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.640
The conventional straight razor carried an idiot index of perhaps 50 or higher, once labor,

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.220
grinding time and retail markup were added.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:25.020
To arrive at that index, divide the several dollar retail price by the few cents of steel

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.640
inside each finished razor.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:32.860
The ratio lands in the range of 50 or more, because nearly all the value sat in the repeated

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.240
handed operations rather than the metal itself.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:40.740
Gillette's move was to accept that the blade would be thrown away after limited use, and

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.020
therefore could be made far thinner and simpler.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.920
Progressive die stamping replaced forging and hand grinding.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:53.640
The dies cut the blank, form the edge geometry, and harden the steel in a continuous high-speed line.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:03:02.480
Because the blade no longer had to survive repeated honing, the edge could be ground once at the factory to a sharpness that lasted for a handful of shapes.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:11.260
The holder itself became a simple clamp rather than a precision frame, shifting almost all the cost and complexity into the replaceable part.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:17.220
Each of these steps removed either material, labor hours, or the need for skilled re-sharpening,

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.060
driving the finished price steadily closer to the steel cost itself.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.180
The first die station shared rectangular blanks from the strip,

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.020
removing the need for individual forging heats.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:32.900
Subsequent stations coined the central slots that would later engage the holder clamp

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:37.100
and simultaneously coined the two cutting edges to a preliminary bevel.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:42.400
A final station applied a rapid quench that left the thin steel at the required hardness

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.300
without the slow annealing cycle required for a reusable razor.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:53.220
Because the blade was now only 0.1mm thick instead of several millimetres, the massive steel

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.180
per cutting edge dropped by roughly an order of magnitude.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:02.180
The holder, no longer required to protect a delicate long life edge, could be stamped from

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:07.860
mild steel or even cast in simple two-piece form, eliminating the need for precision alignment

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.460
features that had added weight and machining steps to earlier designs.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.740
One potential objection is that a thinner blade might flex or chatter during use.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.040
Gillette addressed this by tensioning the blade between the clamp plates so that the unsupported

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:24.080
span remained short enough for stiffness.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:26.500
Another objection is safety.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:32.020
A disposable edge might be sharper on first use, but could nick more easily if the user

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.460
applied the same pressure as with a straight razor.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:40.340
The three-piece safety guard built into the holder limited the blade exposure angle, converting

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:45.800
the higher initial sharpness into a controlled cutting depth rather than an uncontrolled risk.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:51.300
Within roughly 20 years the retail price per blade had fallen until the margin above raw

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.420
steel was modest rather than dramatic.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:58.780
The safety razor system created a recurring purchase that matched the physics of a thin,

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:00.360
single-use cutting edge.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:09.760
The remaining gap above the magic wand floor now sits mainly in packaging, distribution and brand, not in the metal or the stamping operation.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:20.140
Tradeoffs remain. The thin stamped blade cannot match the longevity or the shaving feel of a properly maintained straight razor for users willing to invest the skill in time.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:26.920
Modern equivalents still show that the steel itself accounts for only a small fraction of the packaged retail price,

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:35.380
confirming that the original cost reduction was achieved by redesigning the entire usage cycle rather than by squeezing the material cost alone.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:41.100
The system also created a new dependency on consistent high-volume diamantments.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:49.900
Any interruption in the stamping line would halt blade supply, whereas a straight-raiser maker could continue production with hand tools even at low volume.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:55.820
A consumable part whose price once reflected hand craftsmanship can be driven toward its

00:05:55.820 --> 00:06:00.180
material cost once the entire system is redesigned around frequent replacement.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:05.340
The same logic appears whenever an industry treats a short-lived component as if it must

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:12.000
endure like a capital good, which other everyday items still carry an idiot index set by yesterday's

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.640
assumptions about durability rather than today's stamping and forming capabilities.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.860
That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:23.100
The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.640
